Disclaimer
------------------------------------------------------------

    At the moment, tuna is in public testing, so the
architecture and the handbook may experience significant
changes.  You can help development by contributing or
opening an issue!


Introduction
------------------------------------------------------------

    tuna is a tiny, header-only framework for game
development in C++, implementing a familiar game world
architecture in the OOP paradigm, written according to UNIX
philosophy.

    Due to the minimalist nature of the framework, you will
spend time implementing some basic things yourself.  Such is
the price of freedom!

    Despite its size, this handbook is recommended reading.
It is not a complete class reference, but rather a
compilation of recommendations and solutions that will help
using tuna.

    Detailed API reference is the source code itself.


Installation
------------------------------------------------------------

    Be aware of license terms!  tuna is licensed under MPL
2.0 — it is compatible with other open or closed licenses,
but any changes must stay open.  Read: https://mozilla.org/MPL/2.0/

    You can download tuna in one of these simple ways.

    1.  Download tuna.hh and put it into project include.

    2.  Clone or make this repository a module, then add it
        to project include path.  Suitable if you want to
        update tuna often.

    tuna uses C++20 features and cannot be compiled on C++17
and so on.


Model
------------------------------------------------------------

    Script:
            Script is a basic logic unit of every game!
        tuna has this unit too and it is mandatory for
        development.

            tuna has no default script methods so you can
        pick them yourself!

        class Script : public tuna::Script {
        public:
            virtual void loop(float DELTA_TIME) { (void)DELTA_TIME; }
            virtual void step(float FIXED_DELTA_TIME) { (void)FIXED_DELTA_TIME; }
        };

            Now all your scripts should inherit from this
        class.

    Object:
            Scripts don't hang in the air, they need a
        container.  That is the object.

        std::weak_ptr obj = world.create();

            It has methods for managing granted scripts.

        if(auto ptr = obj.lock()) {
            ptr->grant<SomeComponent>(123, "abc", player);  // Grant the object a script
            ptr->take<SomeComponent>();                     // Remove the component from the object
            std::weak_ptr a = ptr->seek<FooComponent>();    // Seek for component
            // auto it = ptr->find<FooComponent>();         // There is also a search that returns iterator
            ptr->clean();                                   // Clean all scripts
        }

     World:
            The center of the universe.  A container for
        objects and the game loop manager.

         std::weak_ptr obj = world.create();                // Can create objects
         world.kill(obj);                                   // Can KILL objects!
         world.dispatch<Script, &Script::loop>(delta_time); // Is used to implement the game loop
         world.clean();                                     // Clean up the world


Solutions
------------------------------------------------------------

    Locked Pointer:
            If e.g std::weak_ptr::lock suddenly returns
        nullptr, there is little you can do about it.
        tuna::locked_ptr is one of the solutions to that.
        When it gets nullptr from a weak_ptr it terminates
        the program.

    Snapshots:
            There is no such type as Scene in tuna.  No
        interface that they can be made with neither.  BUT
        there is C++, and some macros in tuna that provide
        you a simple way to make them.

        TUNA_NEW_SNAPSHOT(Main) {
            world.clean(); // So that it reloads when called again
            ...
            return 0; // Returning int as a status
        }

        TUNA_LOAD_SNAPSHOT(Main, world);

        TUNA_SNAPSHOT(Main) // This macros lets you access the underlying function identifier

    Samples:
            Simillary to scenes, prefabs are implemented via
        macros too.

        TUNA_NEW_SAMPLE(Player, tuna::locked_ptr<tuna::Object> obj) {
            obj->grant<Some>(controller);
            ...
            return 0; // Returning int as a status
        }

        TUNA_LOAD_SAMPLE(Player, obj_player);

        TUNA_SAMPLE(Player) // This macros lets you access the underlying function identifier

    References:
            You cannot search by scripts or names in tuna,
        but that's not a problem!  You already create all
        scripts in a single scope so you can pass references
        dirrectly from there.

        TUNA_NEW_SNAPSHOT(Main) {
            world.clean();

            tuna::locked_ptr obj = world.create(); {
                tuna::locked_ptr transform = obj->grant<Transform>();
                tuna::locked_ptr player = obj->grant<Player>(transform);
            }

            return 0;
        }


Don'ts
------------------------------------------------------------

    Manually mutating tuna::World::objects and
tuna::Object::scripts leads to UB.

    Calling tuna::World::dispatch will work (and make sense)
only in the main loop.

    Storing object and script references via std::shared_ptr
leads to invalid lifetimes.

    tuna::Object::grant will silently ignore duplicated
script granting.

    Mutating tuna::World in script constructors/destructors
leads to UB.

    Mixing or using base types of script will lead to UB.

    tuna::locked_ptr is only applicable in local scopes.
Storing variables of this type globally or in
long-presenting objects will lead to invalid lifetimes
and UB.


Implementation
------------------------------------------------------------

    Now as we learnt a lot, we can proceed to implementing
an empty project with tuna!

#include "tuna.hh" // tuna!
#include <draw> // Le "draw" as a renderer library

class Component : public tuna::Script { // Project main script type
public:
    virtual void Update(float delta_time) { (void) delta_time; } // Methods with a value
    virtual void Draw() { }
};

struct Sprite : public Component { // A full-fledged script
    draw::Vector2 position;
    draw::Color tint;

    Sprite(draw::Vector2 ipos, draw::Color itint) // Applying constructor for script initalisation
        : position(ipos), tint(itint) { }

    void Draw() override { // Implementing script logic
        draw::square(position, {1, 1}, tint);
    }
};

struct Mover : public Component {
    std::weak_ptr<Sprite> wsprite; // References

    Mover(std::weak_ptr<Sprite> isprite) : wsprite(isprite) { } // Fulfiling the dependency via constructor

    void Update(float delta_time) override {
        tuna::locked_ptr sprite = wsprite; // Using locked_ptr
        sprite->position.x += 10.0f * delta_time;
    }
};

TUNA_NEW_SAMPLE(MovingSquare, tuna::locked_ptr<tuna::Object> iobj, draw::Vector2 ipos, draw::Color itint) {
    iobj->clean();
    // Passing reference in place
    tuna::locked_ptr lsprite = iobj->grant<Sprite>(ipos, itint);
    tuna::locked_ptr lmover = iobj->grant<Mover>(lsprite); // locked_ptr can be used as a validation
    return 0;
}

TUNA_NEW_SNAPSHOT(Main) { // Main scene
    world.clean();

    // Making a scene with code!
    world.create().lock()->grant<Sprite>(draw::Vector2 {-2, -2}, draw::RED);
    world.create().lock()->grant<Sprite>(draw::Vector2 {2, 2}, draw::BLUE);
    world.create().lock()->grant<Sprite>(draw::Vector2 {4, 4}, draw::GREEN);
    TUNA_LOAD_SAMPLE(MovingSquare, world.create(), draw::Vector2 {-2, 0}, draw::YELLOW); // Applying samples

    return 0;
}

int main() {
    tuna::World world;

    TUNA_LOAD_SNAPSHOT(Main, world);

    while(draw::window(400, 400, "Tuna!")) {
        world.dispatch<Component, &Component::Update>(draw::delta_time()); // Type of the base script and method that has to be called on all objects

        draw::begin_drawing();
            draw::fill(draw::Color::SKY_BLUE);
            world.dispatch<Component, &Component::Draw>();
        draw::end_drawing();
    }

    world.clean(); // Cleanup
    return 0;
}


